Hmm — `await bloc.close()` waits for processing of the event; expectLater's future should be awaited too. Order: expectLater returns a Future that completes when the stream matches. bloc.close() processes the event before closing (bloc drains... actually close() cancels subscriptions and processes pending events? In bloc, close waits for... Bloc.close cancels the onEvent... Let me think: bloc's close() → awaits `_closeSubject`... Actually the standard blocTest awaits `bloc.close()` after adding events and expects emitted states to have processed. In bloc v8/v9, `close()` returns a Future that completes when the bloc is closed AND pending event handlers complete (it awaits emit.done). Yes — close processes queued events first.

Safer deterministic approach:
```dart
final states = <CollectionState>[];
final subscription = bloc.stream.listen(states.add);
bloc.add(const CollectionStarted());
await bloc.close();
expect(states, [CollectionLoadInProgress(), CollectionLoadSuccess(cats)]);
``
Hmm — will `listen` capture states emitted before... The event handler runs async; states emitted after listen. close() awaits handler completion, then the stream is done — subscription receives all. But is there a race: last state emitted synchronously before close completes — delivered via stream asynchronously; after close() the stream emits done; listener got everything? bloc.close: cancels... For broadcast stream (bloc.stream is a broadcast wrapper), states added go to the underlying subject; the listen subscription attached before emissions receives them. close() → `await _blocStatesSubject.close()` flushes. Listener callbacks are sync on delivery — after subject closes, done event delivered. So after `await bloc.close()`, all states delivered. This is the common manual pattern. 

Even more deterministic alternative: don't add via event; call... no, keep the event-based pattern with subscription. Actually the expectLater + emitsInOrder + `await bloc.close()` might race (close before expectLater subscription... expectLater subscribes immediately upon call — it returns a future but the subscription is created synchronously inside). Both patterns work; I'll use the explicit subscription pattern — clearer for readers.

